      "role": "assistant",
      "content": "Yes, this \"accentuation bias\" is well-documented, where clinicians ignore symptoms that deviate from the prototype phenotype common in early training textbooks. For conditions like lupus or atypical cardiovascular presentations, this leads to less time spent distinguishing between numerous differential diagnoses on minority patients compared to their white counterparts. Ultimately, this bias, combined with the difficulty of visualizing inflammation on darker skin, creates a significant barrier to accurate diagnosis and treatment.",
